
Your query is mostly correct and will work, but there are a few things you can simplify and improve for performance and clarity. I’ll explain the important parts and suggest a cleaner version.


---

1. Issue with COALESCE(m.TapOut, NULL)

This part:

COALESCE(m.TapOut, NULL) AS TapOut

is unnecessary.

COALESCE(x, NULL) will always return x, because NULL does not change anything.

So this:

COALESCE(m.TapOut, NULL)

is exactly the same as:

m.TapOut

✔ Replace with:

m.TapOut


---

2. Join Condition (RFID Matching)

Your join logic is correct because you support:

1️⃣ New RFID (first 10 characters of EM)
2️⃣ Old RFID from vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo

But using OR EXISTS inside a JOIN can sometimes slow down queries if the tables are large.

Your logic:

(CASE 
    WHEN LEN(h.EM) > 10 THEN SUBSTRING(h.EM,1,10)
    ELSE h.EM
END = m.CardSerialNo)

OR EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo v
    WHERE v.APOAccount = h.APOAccount
    AND v.CardSerialNo = m.CardSerialNo
)

This is valid, but just be aware it may impact performance if your tables grow large.


---

3. Better Query Structure

I recommend this cleaned version.

SELECT 
    mt.MeetingId,
    mt.Title,
    mt.StartDateTime,
    mt.EndDateTime,

    h.EmployeeNo,
    CONCAT(h.FirstName, ' ', COALESCE(h.MiddleName, ''), ' ', h.LastName) AS FullName,
    h.Grade,
    CONCAT(h.DivisionName, ' / ', h.DepartmentName, ' / ', h.SectionName) AS Department,

    m.TapIn,
    m.TapOut

FROM tbl_participants m

LEFT JOIN [db_EPPIIIP_New].[dbo].[TBL_M_USERMASTER] h
    ON 
        (
            CASE 
                WHEN LEN(h.EM) > 10 THEN SUBSTRING(h.EM,1,10)
                ELSE h.EM
            END = m.CardSerialNo
        )
        OR EXISTS
        (
            SELECT 1
            FROM [db_EPPIIIP_New].[dbo].[vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo] v
            WHERE v.APOAccount = h.APOAccount
            AND v.CardSerialNo = m.CardSerialNo
        )

LEFT JOIN tbl_meetings mt
    ON m.MeetingId = mt.MeetingId

WHERE mt.CreatedBy = @CreatedBy

ORDER BY m.Id DESC;


---

4. Small Improvement (FullName spacing issue)

If MiddleName is NULL you will get double spaces.

Example:

John  Doe

Better version:

CONCAT(
    h.FirstName, ' ',
    ISNULL(h.MiddleName + ' ', ''),
    h.LastName
) AS FullName


